PLACES=[
N('nhm-1913-building-los-angeles','Natural History Museum of Los Angeles County (the 1913 Building)','Los Angeles','ca','inst','standing',34.01713,-118.28942,'site','open','drive','','1910–1913',
  'Built as the Los Angeles County Museum of History, Science and Art. Ground was broken in July 1910, the park around it was renamed Exposition Park at the cornerstone laying that December, and the museum opened in November 1913, in the same days the city celebrated the opening of the Los Angeles Aqueduct. The museum grew outward from this first building over the years; the 1913 Building was renovated for two years and reopened in 2009, with its stained glass skylight restored.',
  'The domed rotunda of 1913, lined with marble columns under a stained glass dome, with the first public artwork the county paid for: Julia Bracken Wendt\'s statue Three Muses (History, Science and Art). The 1913 Building now holds the Age of Mammals, Dinosaur Hall and Becoming Los Angeles halls.',
  'The first museum building in Exposition Park, opened the week the aqueduct water arrived.',
  'Run by the Natural History Museums of Los Angeles County in Exposition Park. Admission charged; free for county residents 3 to 5 p.m. on weekdays (bought at the museum), the museum\'s visit page says (read November 2026). Parking in Exposition Park is paid; prices on the museum\'s parking page.',
  [W('Natural History Museum of Los Angeles County'),['Natural History Museum: visit','https://nhm.org/visit']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='Daily 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed the first Tuesday of each month and some holidays'),

N('fine-arts-building-los-angeles','Fine Arts Building','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.04909,-118.25912,'exact','view','walk','','1927–',
  'A twelve-storey office block of 1927 at 811 West 7th Street, designed by Albert R. Walker and Percy A. Eisen and laid out like a Renaissance palace in three bands. It was declared a Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument in 1974; it is also known as the Global Marine House.',
  'A smooth pale stone front whose lowest three storeys carry a two-storey arched entrance crowded with terracotta: acanthus scrolls, rope circles and small columns carved with fantastic creatures, and a tympanum of sculpture at the top. Inside, a two-storey arched lobby with a shallow pool and bronze figures by Burt Johnson, ceramic relief panels and seventeen bronze and glass showcases. Whether the lobby is open to passers-by was not checked.',
  'A downtown office lobby dressed like a cathedral treasury.',
  'A private office building on 7th Street; look from the pavement. Lobby access not checked.',
  [W('Fine Arts Building (Los Angeles)')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('hotel-alexandria-los-angeles','Hotel Alexandria','Los Angeles','ca','hotel','standing',34.0474,-118.25013,'exact','view','walk','','1906–',
  'The first luxury hotel in downtown Los Angeles, at 501 to 511 South Spring Street, designed by John B. Parkinson and opened in February 1906 as an eight-storey building; a twelve-storey addition behind it followed in 1911. Early film people stayed here, among them Mary Pickford, Charlie Chaplin and Rudolph Valentino. It declined over the century and was turned into apartments in 2005; the whole building became a Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument in 2013.',
  'Inside the 1911 addition is the Palm Court ballroom with its stained glass ceiling, restored after 2014 along with the second-floor ballroom and parts of the lobby. The building is a contributing part of the Spring Street Financial District. It is apartments and an event space now, so the inside is seen only at events.',
  'Los Angeles\'s first grand hotel, where the silent-film crowd stayed.',
  'A private apartment building at 5th and Spring streets; look from the street. The Palm Court is hired out for events and filming.',
  [W('Hotel Alexandria')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('bullocks-wilshire-los-angeles','Bullocks Wilshire','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.06156,-118.2884,'exact','view','drive','','1929–',
  'An Art Deco department store of 1929 at 3050 Wilshire Boulevard, built by John G. Bullock as the luxury flagship of his chain and designed by John and Donald Parkinson. It was among the first stores in Los Angeles built for shoppers who came by car, in what was then mostly a residential district. The chain passed to I. Magnin in 1989 and closed in 1994, the year Southwestern Law School, its neighbour, bought the building.',
  'A 241-foot tower whose top is sheathed in copper, gone green. Over the Wilshire entrance is a stylised relief by George Stanley, who designed the Oscar statuette, and the porte-cochère ceiling has a mural by Herman Sachs. The law school restored the building to its 1929 state and uses it as part of its campus.',
  'The 1929 store built for people arriving by car, now a law school.',
  'Owned by Southwestern Law School and used as part of its campus; look from Wilshire Boulevard. Visits inside not checked.',
  [W('Bullocks Wilshire')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('biltmore-hotel-los-angeles','The Biltmore Los Angeles (Biltmore Hotel)','Los Angeles','ca','hotel','standing',34.04988,-118.254,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1923–',
  'Opened in October 1923 by the Bowman-Biltmore chain, opposite Pershing Square, as the largest hotel in the United States west of Chicago, with 1,500 rooms (683 today). Schultze and Weaver drew its blend of Spanish-Italian Renaissance and Beaux-Arts styles. It became a city Historic-Cultural Monument in 1969, was restored in the late 1970s, was named the Millennium Biltmore in 2001 and the Biltmore Los Angeles in 2024.',
  'Inside: frescoed ceilings in the Galleria and the Crystal Ballroom, painted in 1922 by the Italian artist John B. Smeraldi; the old main lobby, now the Rendezvous Court, with a gilded Moorish Revival plaster ceiling, chandeliers of 1923 and a bronze doorway whose astronomical clock still keeps time; travertine walls, oak panelling and bronze stairways.',
  'The 1923 grand hotel whose ballroom ceilings were painted by hand.',
  'A working hotel facing Pershing Square. Afternoon tea is served in the Rendezvous Court. Hours of the public rooms were not checked.',
  [W('The Biltmore Los Angeles')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='?'),

N('los-angeles-city-hall','Los Angeles City Hall','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.05368,-118.24281,'exact','open','walk','','1926–1928',
  'Designed by John Parkinson, John C. Austin and Albert C. Martin Sr., completed in 1928 and dedicated on April 26 that year. At 454 feet and 32 floors it was the tallest building in the city until 1966; a height limit of the day did not apply to it as a public building. Its tower was modelled on the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus. A seismic retrofit from 1998 to 2001 set it on base isolators.',
  'The stepped white tower that has been on Los Angeles police badges since 1940. Wikipedia says the concrete of the tower was mixed with sand from every county in California and water from its 21 missions. A free observation deck on the 27th floor is open to the public on weekdays; the Tom Bradley Room is used for ceremonies.',
  'The tower on every LAPD badge, with a free view from the 27th floor.',
  'The seat of city government, on the block of Main, Temple, First and Spring streets. The observation deck is free on weekdays in business hours (Wikipedia); the exact hours and the entry rules were not read on a City of Los Angeles page.',
  [W('Los Angeles City Hall')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='?'),

N('herald-examiner-building-los-angeles','Los Angeles Herald Examiner Building','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.03943,-118.25949,'exact','view','walk','','1913–1914',
  'Built for William Randolph Hearst\'s Los Angeles Examiner on land he bought in 1913 at the southwest corner of Broadway and 11th Street, finished in 1914 in the Mission and Spanish Colonial Revival styles, largely to designs by Julia Morgan with the Los Angeles architects J. Martyn Haenke and William J. Dodd. The paper, merged into the Herald Examiner in 1962, closed in 1989. For the next 26 years the building was a much-used film set; a developer, Georgetown, took it over in 2015 and renovated it for shops and offices.',
  'A Mission Revival front whose arched ground-floor windows were covered over for years after vandalism. It is a Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument. What of the old lobby can be seen inside was not checked.',
  'Julia Morgan\'s newspaper palace for Hearst, later a film set for 26 years.',
  'Private, on Broadway at 11th Street; look from the street. Shops on the ground floor were planned in the renovation; whether they are open was not checked.',
  [W('Los Angeles Herald Examiner')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('farmers-merchants-bank-los-angeles','Farmers and Merchants Bank Building','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.04807,-118.24755,'exact','view','walk','','1905–',
  'The building of the Farmers and Merchants Bank, the first incorporated bank in Los Angeles (founded 1871), at Fourth and Main. Morgan and Walls designed it in 1905 in the Classical Revival style, as a bank \"temple\". It worked as a bank until the late 1980s and is Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument 271.',
  'A two-storey front like a Roman temple, with Corinthian columns at the entrance under a large pediment. Much of the old banking hall remains inside: the light fittings, a central skylight and the loggia with its railings. It is now mainly an events hall and film location, so the inside is seen at events.',
  'A columned 1905 bank temple at Fourth and Main.',
  'Privately owned events hall and film location; look from Main and Fourth streets.',
  [W('Farmers and Merchants Bank of Los Angeles'),['Los Angeles Conservancy: Farmers and Merchants Bank and Annexes','https://www.laconservancy.org/locations/farmers-and-merchants-bank-and-annexes']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('million-dollar-theatre-los-angeles','Million Dollar Theatre','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.05087,-118.24846,'exact','view','walk','','1917–',
  'One of the first movie palaces in the United States, at 307 South Broadway, built by Sid Grauman and opened in 1917 (Wikipedia gives both 1917 and 1918 for it). From 1949, under Frank Fouce, it was the leading Spanish-language theatre and cinema in the country, and the Spanish-language television that became KMEX traces its roots to it. After years as a church and then empty, it reopened in 2008. It is on the National Register.',
  'Joseph Mora\'s Spanish Colonial Revival front of riotous Churrigueresque ornament, with statues and longhorn skulls, across the street from the Bradbury Building. William L. Woollett designed the auditorium. The office tower above has been turned into apartments.',
  'Sid Grauman\'s first movie palace, later the heart of Spanish-language show business.',
  'Open inside only for shows and events booked there; look from Broadway the rest of the time.',
  [W('Million Dollar Theatre')],
  conf='mixed', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('angels-flight-los-angeles','Angels Flight','Los Angeles','ca','rail','standing',34.05134,-118.25021,'site','open','walk','inclines','1901–',
  'A narrow-gauge funicular with two cars, Olivet and Sinai, which pull against each other on one cable up Bunker Hill. It ran beside the Third Street Tunnel from 1901 until 1969, when its site was cleared for redevelopment, and the cars and station pieces were stored. It was rebuilt half a block south in 1996, between 3rd and 4th streets from Hill Street up to California Plaza. It closed after a fatal accident in 2001 and again after a derailment in 2013, and reopened in August 2017.',
  'The two original cars, Olivet and Sinai, and the station pieces brought back from storage, on a short steep track from Hill Street. You can ride it.',
  'The 1901 Bunker Hill funicular, still running on a new site.',
  'Runs as a public utility under a city franchise. The fare was $1.50 one way from June 2025, Wikipedia says; the operator\'s page would not open here, so hours and the current fare were not checked.',
  [W('Angels Flight'),['Angels Flight Railway','https://www.angelsflight.org/']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='?'),

N('pacific-electric-building-los-angeles','Pacific Electric Building','Los Angeles','ca','rail','standing',34.04479,-118.25018,'exact','view','walk','stations','1905–',
  'Opened in January 1905 at 6th and Main as the main station and headquarters of Henry Huntington\'s Pacific Electric Railway, designed by Thornton Fitzhugh. Main Street Station on its ground floor served the interurban trains to the south and east; in 1914 more than 1,600 trains a day came or went. Five floors of offices sat above the station and the Jonathan Club filled the top three. For decades its ten floors held more floor space than any building west of Chicago.',
  'A ten-storey block whose old waiting room and bus concourse became a parking garage once rail service ended. The building was turned into live-work lofts in 2005. Its pilasters are kept on the front.',
  'Huntington\'s interurban terminal of 1905, now lofts.',
  'Private lofts at 6th and Main streets; look from the street.',
  [W('Pacific Electric Building')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('los-angeles-central-library-goodhue','Los Angeles Central Library (Goodhue Building)','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.05028,-118.25504,'exact','open','walk','','1924–1926',
  'The city\'s main library, designed by Bertram Goodhue, built from 1924 to 1926 and opened in July 1926. Two arson fires in 1986 destroyed a fifth of the collection, then the contents of the music reading room, and led to a renovation and the Tom Bradley wing of 1993. The Goodhue Building has been on the National Register since 1970.',
  'The central tower is capped by a tiled pyramid with suns on its sides and a hand holding the torch of learning at the top. Inside: the rotunda with Dean Cornwell\'s four-part mural of California history (finished about 1933), figures, chandeliers and grilles. Maguire Gardens are on the west side.',
  'Goodhue\'s library with the tiled pyramid, rebuilt after the 1986 fires.',
  'Free, run by the Los Angeles Public Library at 630 West 5th Street. Hours from the library\'s own page, read November 2026; closed on some holidays.',
  [W('Los Angeles Central Library'),['Los Angeles Public Library: Central Library','https://www.lapl.org/branches/central-library']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='Monday to Thursday 10 a.m. to 8 p.m.; Friday and Saturday 9:30 a.m. to 5:30 p.m.; Sunday 1 to 5 p.m.'),

N('st-vincent-de-paul-church-los-angeles','St. Vincent de Paul Catholic Church','Los Angeles','ca','worship','standing',34.0289,-118.27631,'exact','open','walk','','1924–1925',
  'The parish church of St. Vincent\'s, the third parish founded in the city, which grew out of the chapel of St. Vincent\'s College (1865). Edward L. Doheny and his wife paid for the new church on condition, the parish says, that it be modelled on the cathedrals of Mexico. The cornerstone was laid in July 1924 and the first Mass said in April 1925, at Adams Boulevard and Figueroa Street. Albert C. Martin Sr. designed it, with the interior by Samuel Lunden for Cram and Ferguson; the Vincentians have run it from the start.',
  'A church on the corner of Adams and Figueroa built to recall the cathedrals of Mexico, Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument 90. Its altar is seen in the final scenes of the film End of Days, and the church also appears in Constantine. What else survives inside was not checked.',
  'The Dohenys\' church in the style of Mexico\'s cathedrals.',
  'An active parish of the Archdiocese of Los Angeles at 621 West Adams Boulevard. Mass times from the archdiocese\'s parish page, read November 2026; Masses mostly in Spanish, the 12 noon Sunday and 12:10 weekday Masses in English.',
  [W('St. Vincent de Paul Catholic Church (Los Angeles)'),['Archdiocese of Los Angeles: St. Vincent Catholic Church','https://lacatholics.org/venue/st-vincent-catholic-church/']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='Mass Sunday 8, 10, 12, 2 and 5; Saturday 7:30 a.m.; weekdays 12:10 p.m. and early morning'),

N('tower-theatre-los-angeles','Tower Theatre','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.04355,-118.25468,'exact','open','walk','','1927–',
  'A movie theatre opened in 1927 on Broadway, the first designed by S. Charles Lee, built for H. L. Gumbiner on the site of the Garrick Theatre and seating 900. It was the first theatre in Los Angeles wired for sound, where The Jazz Singer had its Los Angeles premiere, and the first air-conditioned one. It closed as a cinema in 1988 and became Historic-Cultural Monument 450 the next year. After years empty and as a church, it was restored by Apple and reopened as a shop in June 2021.',
  'A Baroque Revival front at 802 South Broadway with a tall corner tower in the manner of Goodhue; the auditorium inside was modelled on the Paris Opera House. As a shop it can be walked into during opening hours.',
  'The theatre that premiered The Jazz Singer in Los Angeles, now a shop you can walk into.',
  'Open to the public as Apple Tower Theatre, a shop. Hours from Apple\'s own store page, read November 2026; holiday hours differ.',
  [W('Tower Theatre (Los Angeles)'),['Apple Tower Theatre','https://www.apple.com/retail/towertheatre/']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours='Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 8 p.m.; Sunday 10 a.m. to 7 p.m.'),

N('united-artists-theatre-los-angeles','United Artists Theatre','Los Angeles','ca','civic','standing',34.04178,-118.25718,'exact','view','walk','','1927–',
  'A movie palace and office building at 937 South Broadway, built for United Artists, the studio formed by D. W. Griffith, Charlie Chaplin, Douglas Fairbanks and Mary Pickford, and designed by C. Howard Crane with Walker and Eisen in a Spanish Gothic style after Segovia Cathedral. The televangelist Gene Scott held Sunday services in it from 1990 to 2005. The building became the Ace Hotel in 2014, with the theatre restored; the hotel closed in January 2024 and reopened that February as a rooms-only hotel, and the theatre is now called the United Theater on Broadway.',
  'A thirteen-storey Gothic front; the theatre fills three floors, with 2,214 seats in rows that sink toward the stage. Inside only for shows.',
  'Chaplin and Pickford\'s own movie palace, built like a Spanish cathedral.',
  'Private hotel and theatre on Broadway; the theatre opens for ticketed shows. Look from Broadway otherwise.',
  [W('United Artists Theatre (Los Angeles)')],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),

N('immanuel-presbyterian-church-los-angeles','Immanuel Presbyterian Church','Los Angeles','ca','worship','standing',34.06162,-118.29462,'exact','view','drive','','1929–',
  'A congregation founded downtown in 1888 out of First Presbyterian; its present church on Wilshire Boulevard, now in Koreatown, was finished in 1929 to designs by Chauncey Fitch Skilling, in the French Gothic Revival style. At its peak in 1943 it had 4,300 members. It began services in Spanish in 1995 and is known for holding bilingual services. It became a Los Angeles Historic-Cultural Monument in 2003.',
  'A 205-foot corner tower and a rose window over the entrance. Inside, beam trusses, columns and arches, a carved wooden pulpit, chandeliers and oak furnishings, with stained glass by the Dixon Art Glass company and newer windows by Judson Studios.',
  'One of the great Wilshire Boulevard churches of the 1920s, with a 205-foot tower.',
  'An active church on Wilshire Boulevard; look from the street. The church\'s own page gave no service or visiting times when read in November 2026.',
  [W('Immanuel Presbyterian Church (Los Angeles, California)'),['Immanuel Presbyterian Church','https://www.immanuelpres.org/']],
  conf='', th=None, thn=None, brick='?', hours=''),
]

